LESSON 2: FETCHING DATA FROM A SERVER THE WRONG WAY
================================================================================
PART 1: OVERVIEW OF THE LESSON
================================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL LEARN IN THIS LESSON
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This lesson focuses on common mistakes when fetching backend data in React.

You will understand:

• Why fetching during render is dangerous

• How repeated Effects can create excessive requests

• When an omitted dependency array is actually a problem

• How dependency-driven loops happen

• How unstable object/function dependencies can retrigger Effects

• Why state objects are different from objects created during render

• Why obsolete requests can cause race conditions

• Why request cancellation can be useful

• How missing error handling affects the UI

• Why response.ok must be checked

• How loading states can become stuck

• How to identify network-request problems

• How to debug Effect execution

• How to recognize incorrect fetching patterns


WHY THIS LESSON IS IMPORTANT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Backend communication introduces several moving parts:

React Render
     ↓
Effect
     ↓
HTTP Request
     ↓
Backend
     ↓
HTTP Response
     ↓
State Update
     ↓
Render


If these steps are organized incorrectly, you can create:

• Repeated HTTP requests

• Infinite state/effect loops

• Stale data

• Race conditions

• Stuck loading indicators

• Unhandled errors

• Obsolete requests

• Incorrect UI state


The goal of this lesson is:

UNDERSTAND THE FAILURE MODES

before implementing the correct backend pattern.


================================================================================
PART 2: UNDERSTANDING THE WRONG WAYS
================================================================================

COMMON DATA-FETCHING PROBLEMS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Calling fetch() during render

2. Running a fetching Effect more often than intended

3. Creating an Effect/state feedback loop

4. Using unstable dependencies

5. Ignoring race conditions

6. Not cancelling obsolete requests where useful

7. Ignoring network errors

8. Ignoring HTTP error responses

9. Leaving loading state stuck

10. Updating the UI with an old response

11. Duplicating fetching logic

12. Using Effects where event handlers are sufficient


================================================================================
IMPORTANT DISTINCTIONS
================================================================================

These statements are TOO BROAD:

"An Effect without a dependency array is always wrong."

"Any object dependency creates an infinite loop."

"Every fetch Effect must have cleanup."

"Every state update after unmount causes a memory leak."

"fetch() rejects for HTTP 404 and 500."

"Unhandled Promise rejection always crashes the application."


A better approach is to understand WHY each pattern can become problematic.


================================================================================
PROBLEM SUMMARY
================================================================================

+------------------------+--------------------------------------------+
| PROBLEM                | POSSIBLE RESULT                            |
+------------------------+--------------------------------------------+
| Fetch during render    | Repeated requests/render impurity          |
| Effect every render    | Repeated requests if Effect fetches        |
| Effect feedback loop   | Continuous rerender/request cycle          |
| Unstable dependency    | Effect runs more often than intended       |
| No HTTP check          | 404/500 treated like successful response   |
| No error handling      | Rejected Promise / poor error UI           |
| No request control     | Obsolete response can win race             |
| No loading handling    | Stuck or misleading loading UI             |
| No cleanup when needed | Stale listeners/connections/requests       |
+------------------------+--------------------------------------------+


================================================================================
PART 3: MISTAKE 1 - DIRECT API CALL DURING RENDER
================================================================================

WRONG PATTERN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

File:

src/components/WrongWay1.jsx


import {
  useState
} from 'react'


export default function WrongWay1() {

  const [
    data,
    setData
  ] = useState(null)


  const [
    loading,
    setLoading
  ] = useState(true)


  // WRONG:
  // Side effect started during render.

  fetch(
    '/api/data'
  )
    .then(
      response =>
        response.json()
    )
    .then(
      result => {

        setData(
          result
        )

        setLoading(
          false
        )

      }
    )


  if (loading) {

    return (
      <p>
        Loading...
      </p>
    )
  }


  return (

    <h1>
      {data?.name}
    </h1>

  )
}


================================================================================
WHAT'S WRONG?
================================================================================

The component function is part of React rendering.

Rendering should calculate:

WHAT THE UI SHOULD LOOK LIKE


It should not start backend synchronization every time it runs.


Possible flow:

Render
  ↓
fetch()
  ↓
Response
  ↓
setData()
  ↓
Render
  ↓
fetch()
  ↓
Response
  ↓
State Update / Render
  ↓
More Requests


Even when React can avoid some identical state updates, fetching during render
remains the wrong architecture because rendering can occur multiple times for
many reasons.


================================================================================
IMPORTANT CORRECTION
================================================================================

Avoid claiming:

"Calling fetch in render ALWAYS creates a strict infinite loop."


The exact behavior depends on:

• Which state values are changed

• Whether React bails out of identical updates

• Parent renders

• Development behavior

• Concurrent rendering behavior


The fundamental issue is simpler:

RENDER MUST REMAIN PURE.

NETWORK REQUESTS SHOULD NOT BE STARTED AS A RENDER SIDE EFFECT.


================================================================================
WHY THIS IS DANGEROUS
================================================================================

Rendering can happen because:

• Local state changes

• Parent renders

• Context changes

• Props change

• Development checks

• Other React rendering behavior


You do not want each render attempt to mean:

SEND ANOTHER HTTP REQUEST


================================================================================
BETTER MENTAL MODEL
================================================================================

WRONG:

Render
 ↓
Network


CORRECT DIRECTION:

Render
 ↓
Commit
 ↓
Effect
 ↓
Network


when fetching is synchronization with an external resource.


================================================================================
PART 4: MISTAKE 2 - UNINTENDED EFFECT AFTER EVERY RENDER
================================================================================

CONDITIONALLY WRONG PATTERN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

useEffect(() => {

  fetchData()

})


There is no dependency array.


This means the Effect runs after every committed render.


IMPORTANT:

THIS IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY A BUG.


It becomes a problem when the Effect performs work that should not happen
after every render.


================================================================================
FETCH EXAMPLE
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const fetchData =
    async () => {

      const response =
        await fetch(
          '/api/data'
        )


      const result =
        await response.json()


      setData(
        result
      )
    }


  fetchData()

})


Potential cycle:

Render
 ↓
Effect
 ↓
Fetch
 ↓
setData()
 ↓
Render
 ↓
Effect
 ↓
Fetch
 ↓
...


================================================================================
WHY "MISSING DEPENDENCY ARRAY" IS AN OVERSIMPLIFICATION
================================================================================

This code is legal:

useEffect(() => {

  console.log(
    'Rendered'
  )

})


The issue is not syntax.

The question is:

SHOULD THIS EFFECT RUN AFTER EVERY RENDER?


For an initial data load:

usually:

NO


Therefore you would typically use an Effect whose dependencies reflect the
resource being synchronized.


================================================================================
FETCH WITH NO CHANGING DEPENDENCY
================================================================================

Conceptually:

useEffect(() => {

  loadInitialData()

}, [])


But remember from Lesson 1:

[] does NOT mean:

"Guaranteed exactly one call under every development configuration."


Development Strict Mode can intentionally perform additional setup/cleanup
cycles.


Think:

[] =
NO CHANGING REACTIVE DEPENDENCIES


================================================================================
FETCH DEPENDING ON ID
================================================================================

If the resource depends on:

userId


then:

useEffect(() => {

  loadUser(
    userId
  )

}, [
  userId
])


not:

[]


================================================================================
PART 5: MISTAKE 3 - DEPENDENCY-DRIVEN LOOPS
================================================================================

IMPORTANT CORRECTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The original example claimed this creates a new object every render:

const [
  filters,
  setFilters
] = useState({})


That is NOT correct.


React preserves the current state value between renders.

Therefore:

filters


does NOT automatically become a new object merely because the component
rendered again.


================================================================================
THIS IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY WRONG
================================================================================

const [
  filters,
  setFilters
] = useState({})


useEffect(() => {

  fetchFilteredData(
    filters
  )

}, [
  filters
])


The Effect executes when the stored filters reference changes.


For example:

setFilters({})


creates a new object.


That is a real state change by reference and can legitimately trigger the
Effect.


================================================================================
ACTUAL PROBLEM: OBJECT CREATED DURING RENDER
================================================================================

Consider:

const filters = {

  category:
    selectedCategory

}


useEffect(() => {

  fetchFilteredData(
    filters
  )

}, [
  filters
])


On each render:

filters

is a newly created object.


Conceptually:

Render 1:

filters → Object A


Render 2:

filters → Object B


Even if:

Object A

and:

Object B


contain the same properties:

Object A !== Object B


Therefore the dependency is considered changed.


================================================================================
POTENTIAL LOOP
================================================================================

Render
 ↓
Create Object A
 ↓
Effect
 ↓
Fetch
 ↓
setData()
 ↓
Render
 ↓
Create Object B
 ↓
Dependency Changed
 ↓
Effect
 ↓
Fetch
 ↓
...


================================================================================
UNSTABLE FUNCTION DEPENDENCY
================================================================================

The same idea can apply to functions created during rendering.


Example:

const buildUrl = () => {

  return `/api/data?category=${category}`
}


useEffect(() => {

  fetch(
    buildUrl()
  )

}, [
  buildUrl
])


Each render creates a new:

buildUrl


function reference.


This can cause the Effect to run more often than expected.


================================================================================
BETTER PATTERN 1: DEPEND ON PRIMITIVE VALUES
================================================================================

Instead of:

const filters = {

  category,

  sort

}


useEffect(() => {

  fetchData(
    filters
  )

}, [
  filters
])


prefer when practical:

useEffect(() => {

  fetchData({

    category,

    sort

  })

}, [
  category,
  sort
])


Dependencies:

category

sort


are the actual reactive inputs.


================================================================================
BETTER PATTERN 2: CREATE OBJECT INSIDE EFFECT
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const filters = {

    category,

    sort

  }


  fetchData(
    filters
  )

}, [
  category,
  sort
])


This removes the render-created object from the dependency array.


================================================================================
DON'T REACH FOR useMemo AUTOMATICALLY
================================================================================

You could sometimes stabilize an object with:

useMemo()


But if the object can simply be built inside the Effect:

THAT IS OFTEN EASIER.


Use memoization when it solves a real problem rather than as an automatic fix.


================================================================================
ACTUAL EFFECT LOOP CONDITION
================================================================================

An Effect loop normally requires BOTH:

1. Effect causes a state update

AND

2. That update causes an Effect dependency to differ again


Pattern:

Effect
 ↓
setState()
 ↓
Render
 ↓
Dependency Changes
 ↓
Effect
 ↓
setState()
 ↓
...


================================================================================
PART 6: MISTAKE 4 - NO CONTROL OF OBSOLETE REQUESTS
================================================================================

The biggest fetch-cleanup concern is often:

STALE / OBSOLETE REQUESTS

and:

RACE CONDITIONS


rather than simply:

"setState after unmount always creates a memory leak."


================================================================================
EXAMPLE PROBLEM
================================================================================

Suppose:

userId = 1


Effect starts:

GET /api/users/1


Before it completes:

userId = 2


Now Effect starts:

GET /api/users/2


Network timing:

Request 2
 ↓
finishes quickly


Request 1
 ↓
finishes later


Without protection:

User 2 displayed
      ↓
Old User 1 response arrives
      ↓
User 1 overwrites UI


Result:

STALE DATA


================================================================================
WRONG PATTERN
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const fetchUser =
    async () => {

      const response =
        await fetch(
          `/api/users/${userId}`
        )


      const data =
        await response.json()


      setUser(
        data
      )
    }


  fetchUser()

}, [
  userId
])


There is no cancellation/obsolete-response control.


================================================================================
BETTER PATTERN WITH AbortController
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const controller =
    new AbortController()


  const fetchUser =
    async () => {

      try {

        const response =
          await fetch(
            `/api/users/${userId}`,
            {
              signal:
                controller.signal
            }
          )


        if (!response.ok) {

          throw new Error(
            `Request failed: ${response.status}`
          )
        }


        const data =
          await response.json()


        setUser(
          data
        )


      } catch (error) {

        if (
          error.name ===
          'AbortError'
        ) {

          return
        }


        setError(
          error.message
        )
      }
    }


  fetchUser()


  return () => {

    controller.abort()

  }

}, [
  userId
])


================================================================================
WHAT CLEANUP DOES
================================================================================

When:

userId changes


React cleans up the previous Effect:

controller.abort()


Then the new Effect establishes:

a new request


Flow:

User 1 Request
      ↓
userId changes
      ↓
Cleanup
      ↓
Abort User 1 Request
      ↓
Start User 2 Request


================================================================================
IMPORTANT CORRECTION: RE-RENDER != UNMOUNT
================================================================================

The original lesson described:

User clicks Next User
 ↓
Component re-renders
 ↓
First response updates "UNMOUNTED component"


That is incorrect.


A re-render does NOT mean the component unmounted.


If the same component remains in the tree:

IT IS STILL MOUNTED.


The real issue in this example is:

THE OLD REQUEST MAY BE OBSOLETE


not:

"the component became unmounted because it rendered again."


================================================================================
IMPORTANT MODERN REACT CLARIFICATION
================================================================================

Avoid relying on the old warning:

"Can't perform a React state update on an unmounted component"


as the primary explanation for fetch cancellation.


Modern React's behavior and warnings have evolved.

Cancellation is still valuable because it can:

• Stop obsolete network work

• Avoid stale responses

• Reduce wasted work

• Keep synchronization logic correct


================================================================================
NOT EVERY FETCH ABSOLUTELY REQUIRES ABORT
================================================================================

Do not memorize:

"Every fetch without AbortController is automatically a memory leak."


That is too strong.


Use cancellation when:

• Request can become obsolete

• Dependency changes can start another request

• Component can stop needing the result

• Resource usage matters


The important principle is:

THE UI SHOULD NOT BE UPDATED BY OBSOLETE DATA.


================================================================================
PART 7: MISTAKE 5 - POOR ERROR HANDLING
================================================================================

WRONG PATTERN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

useEffect(() => {

  fetch(
    '/api/data'
  )
    .then(
      response =>
        response.json()
    )
    .then(
      data => {

        setData(
          data
        )

        setLoading(
          false
        )
      }
    )

}, [])


Problems:

• Rejected request isn't handled

• HTTP status isn't checked

• Loading may remain true after failure

• User receives no useful error UI


================================================================================
NETWORK ERROR
================================================================================

Example:

fetch(
  '/api/data'
)


may reject because of:

• Network connection failure

• DNS failure

• Request cancellation

• Certain browser/network failures


Without rejection handling:

Promise rejection
      ↓
No error state
      ↓
Loading may stay true


================================================================================
HTTP 404/500 IS DIFFERENT
================================================================================

Very important:

fetch() does NOT automatically reject merely because the server returned:

404

500

503


Instead:

const response =
  await fetch(
    '/api/data'
  )


Then inspect:

response.ok


Example:

if (!response.ok) {

  throw new Error(
    `Request failed: ${response.status}`
  )
}


================================================================================
BETTER ERROR PATTERN
================================================================================

try {

  const response =
    await fetch(
      '/api/data'
    )


  if (!response.ok) {

    throw new Error(
      `Request failed: ${response.status}`
    )
  }


  const result =
    await response.json()


  setData(
    result
  )


} catch (error) {

  setError(
    error instanceof Error
      ? error.message
      : 'Unable to load data'
  )

}


================================================================================
LOADING STATE
================================================================================

A robust flow should handle:

START

SUCCESS

FAILURE


Example:

try {

  setLoading(
    true
  )

  setError(
    null
  )


  // request


} catch (error) {

  setError(
    ...
  )


} finally {

  setLoading(
    false
  )

}


If using cancellation, be careful not to treat:

AbortError


as a real application failure.


================================================================================
DO UNHANDLED REJECTIONS GUARANTEE AN APP CRASH?
================================================================================

No.


An unhandled Promise rejection is serious and should be fixed.

But saying:

"Unhandled rejection always crashes the React application"


is too absolute.


Possible effects include:

• Console errors

• Broken workflow

• Loading stuck

• Missing error UI

• Environment-dependent failure behavior


The practical rule:

ALWAYS HANDLE EXPECTED REQUEST FAILURES.


